WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.770
Film Courage: So you’re shopping the production
around or you’re looking for a production

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:04.770
company?

00:00:04.770 --> 00:00:08.400
Peter Russell: No, it’s been sold to a production
company but we want to sell it to Netflix,

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.070
we want to sell it to somebody else.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:18.820
I want to be involved in that too (The History
Channel) because I know what a fantastic story

00:00:18.820 --> 00:00:27.179
this is and it’s just the kind of story
that is perfect for a time, for this time.

00:00:27.179 --> 00:00:31.729
African American hero which I think is timely.

00:00:31.729 --> 00:00:44.050
I’m a white guy so I understand that my
kind of guys pretty much run Hollywood for

00:00:44.050 --> 00:00:47.600
a long time, I think it’s time that changed.

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:55.540
But the story theme here (Contrevening the
Rules) is about new heroes.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:01:01.000
So I think it’s about a great chance to
show a real guy, you know?

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.420
I mean his real life was so insanely cool
that you really couldn’t make it up.

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:09.909
So it’s great idea and I know it’s going
to sell.

00:01:09.909 --> 00:01:14.200
Film Courage: Where did you go to start shopping
it around (I know you said it’s already

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:15.200
sold)?

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:16.200
Can you take a step back and tell us?

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:19.999
Peter: Well since it’s happening right now
I can’t tell you exactly who we are going

00:01:19.999 --> 00:01:20.999
to.

00:01:20.999 --> 00:01:22.729
We’re looking at channels.

00:01:22.729 --> 00:01:31.590
We’re looking at channels with names that
have channel in the name and the big companies.

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:41.049
I mean look there are 14 billion dollars now
in the two fields, Internet platform.

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:45.069
14 billion dollars chasing stories right now.

00:01:45.069 --> 00:01:50.560
And it’s funny Suzy says “Don’t say
it’s easy.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:52.649
Not easy to sell anything.

00:01:52.649 --> 00:01:56.909
It’s so hard and you’ve got to be experienced.”

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.170
And that’s all true and this isn’t…Hollywood
is not…there isn’t streets paved with

00:02:01.170 --> 00:02:02.170
gold.

00:02:02.170 --> 00:02:08.310
It’s extremely difficult still to sell anything
in this business (it would be silly to say

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:09.680
that it’s not).

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:18.430
But this is the most exciting time in history
for television at least in the US.

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:23.650
Now I’m going over to South Africa in a
few weeks to lecture at the Durbin Film Market.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:29.720
I’m also getting remarried (well not remarried
but having another ceremony).

00:02:29.720 --> 00:02:37.420
And the South African television market I
think is growing.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:44.040
I’ve just been working with a guy in Brazil
(a big soap opera star) down there who wants

00:02:44.040 --> 00:02:48.440
to do a serial for Netflix.

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:52.630
I think worldwide television is just on fire.

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:57.849
But the interesting thing is this miniseries
about Jack Johnson.

00:02:57.849 --> 00:03:04.690
The miniseries form is probably the closest
to what television offers.

00:03:04.690 --> 00:03:06.780
What do I mean by that?

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:13.840
A miniseries like what you’ll see in the
ASSASSINATION OF GIANI VERSACE and TRUST,

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:20.990
these are long movies rather than tv serials.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:30.210
Now I’ve just worked on a crime drama serial
for television which we can talk about that

00:03:30.210 --> 00:03:31.210
too.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.470
The miniseries form is like a long movie.

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.580
Why is that?

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:41.830
Because you have wounded characters who at
the end of the miniseries have healed or they

00:03:41.830 --> 00:03:46.510
have bled out (one or the other) but they
have an arc and that miniseries arc and that

00:03:46.510 --> 00:03:49.400
miniseries arc is just like a long movie.

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:54.770
So with Jack Johnson (he’s still a hero)
but he still had a journey.

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:58.690
So you get a progression in the story.

00:03:58.690 --> 00:04:04.020
For instance in the ASSASSINATION OF GIANI
VERSACE you’ve got a wounded guy (Andrew

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.290
Cunan was a wounded guy.

00:04:05.290 --> 00:04:10.250
His dad wounded him and his wound never healed)
but he tried to heal.

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:18.170
So you saw this…it was like a 7-episode
long movie so it had a B.M.O.C. which we can

00:04:18.170 --> 00:04:20.320
talk about that too [B.M.O.C = beginning,
middle, obstacle and climax].

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:25.710
So when I’m writing miniseries (if you’re
a movie writer who wants to go into television)

00:04:25.710 --> 00:04:32.280
you should think about as a transition writing
a miniseries because it will give you the

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:39.080
opportunity to use all of your movie chops
but in a television form that lets you transition

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:44.990
into television because you need to have a
character or side characters who are bleeding

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:50.630
at the beginning, who get mentors and wound
rippers all through it and then they learn

00:04:50.630 --> 00:04:56.530
the theme of the show toward the end and they
either heal or die and that’s what a miniseries

00:04:56.530 --> 00:04:57.530
gives you.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.580
Even a serial television series doesn’t
give you.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:03.889
And serial television (obviously) the wounded
person just keeps bleeding all through for

00:05:03.889 --> 00:05:06.060
a hundred episodes.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:12.530
But miniseries offer you the form (a lot of
the forms of a movie) in a television setting.

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:20.030
Film Courage: I know HBO’s SHARP OBJECTS
debuted on Sunday (I didn’t watch it yet)

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:25.340
so it’s a Gillian Flynn, the writer of GONE
GIRL.

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:30.430
I guess she wrote this story (because I started
listening to some interviews with her) before

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:35.050
she wrote GONE GIRL and these other stories
and it’s about a wounded character (who

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:40.140
is a female) and she said “She is very wounded
and has issues, but she’s likable.”

00:05:40.140 --> 00:05:43.440
And she thought it was interesting that it
had to be defended for this character that

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:44.760
she was likable.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:50.110
Because she said “Well, Walter White [BREAKING
BAD] does he have to be likable?

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.270
Tony Soprano [THE SOPRANOS], does he have
to be likable?”

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.300
Peter: I would argue they are likable.

00:05:55.300 --> 00:06:00.199
But sympathetic is actually more important
than likability.

00:06:00.199 --> 00:06:05.700
If you look at Tony Soprano (it’s a good
example), (it’s old now but I think it’s

00:06:05.700 --> 00:06:09.980
the most brilliant television ever made) I
think if you haven’t seen SOPRANOS you should

00:06:09.980 --> 00:06:14.260
watch it because it’s still the most brilliant…it’s
like Shakespeare.

00:06:14.260 --> 00:06:16.070
Tony Soprano is a murderer.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:21.810
He is a horrible human being and yet we love
him, why?

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:31.331
We love him because he’s got this deep wound
in him and it comes from his mother and the

00:06:31.331 --> 00:06:38.419
entire first season of THE SOPRANOS is about
this horrible mother and how awful she is

00:06:38.419 --> 00:06:40.180
to Tony.

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:44.370
And even though when he tries to kill her
on the gurney when they’re rolling her out

00:06:44.370 --> 00:06:47.050
of the nursing home and she’s going “Ha!

00:06:47.050 --> 00:06:48.050
Ha!

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:49.050
Ha!

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:50.050
Ha!

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:51.050
Ha!”

00:06:51.050 --> 00:06:52.050
He’s literally trying to get her with a
pillow.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:53.120
Our sympathies are with Tony, why?

00:06:53.120 --> 00:07:00.229
Well, here’s the big secret of the anti-hero
which I talked about earlier.

00:07:00.229 --> 00:07:08.710
Anti-heroes are great guys who turned bad
for sympathetic reasons.

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:12.350
Michael Corleone becomes a horrible man, why?

00:07:12.350 --> 00:07:14.380
He’s saving his dad?

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:16.400
He’s saving his family.

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:20.130
Tony Soprano is a horrible guy but we like
him, why?

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:23.840
Because he wants to save his family.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:28.720
The first season of Tony Soprano, the ducks
are leaving his pond, his kids are leaving

00:07:28.720 --> 00:07:29.720
the nest.

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:32.960
He’s trying to get his mom saved and into
a home.

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.070
She’s like “Leave me alone.

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.850
You’re a horrible human being.”

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:42.070
So the great secret of the anti-hero is to
make the villain and the people around them

00:07:42.070 --> 00:07:47.139
worse and less likable than the hero themselves.

00:07:47.139 --> 00:07:53.240
Tony Soprano’s mom is the most miserable
human being ever conceived on television and

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.270
he says it’s based on his own mom.

00:07:56.270 --> 00:08:00.009
We love Tony because his mom is just so awful.

00:08:00.009 --> 00:08:04.680
And it’s like poor guy, no wonder he turned
into a murdering psychopath.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:07.020
His mom is awful.

00:08:07.020 --> 00:08:15.570
So that’s the critical thing about making
someone sympathetic and also a likable yes

00:08:15.570 --> 00:08:16.690
(that’s important).

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:21.540
But to make them sympathetic you make the
other people around them worse.

00:08:21.540 --> 00:08:25.780
Michael Corleone had to save his dad nobody
else would do it.

00:08:25.780 --> 00:08:30.350
His brother couldn’t do it, he had these
terrible villains around him that were shooting

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:31.870
his entire family.

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:39.500
He had to save family and in doing so he eventually
lost his family.

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:40.659
Just like Tony Soprano.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:45.890
Tony Soprano ends up killing his nephew, ends
up killing all the people around him.

00:08:45.890 --> 00:08:53.730
Probably ends up having people shot in the
final restaurant scene but we have great sympathy

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:57.160
for him because we see his wounds.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:59.410
His wounds are everywhere.

00:08:59.410 --> 00:09:02.990
Wound are why we love a character.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:07.910
And when you first start out writing a story,
show me your hero’s or character’s wounds

00:09:07.910 --> 00:09:10.620
the second they get on screen.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:15.540
The second they are on screen, show me the
wound that’s how you get someone to be likable.

00:09:15.540 --> 00:09:19.430
You get the note that your character is not
likable (which you get all the time from a

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.149
producer), that’s how to make them likable,
show them bleed.

